A||||11||Phil||Brake the GT||||1||
Z||000000||Phil||02-27-2000||08:44 PM||||My sons 88GT has a brake problem and I'm having trouble solving it. His car has a lot of pedal travel. If you give it a quick pump the pedal will be near the top. The car stops OK. If the car is stopped the second push and every push thereafter the pedal is near the top- move some and the throw is long again. There is no pulsation while stopping and all rotors have been turned and checked with a dial indicator and run true. The calipers slide freely and don't leak. The pads are new. We didn't really realize his car had this problem until I got my Formula. Anybody got any ideas? I noticed that Fiero Store lists a different master cyl for the 88 anybody know the difference or how can you tell them apart?<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 02-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Luperman||02-27-2000||11:02 PM||||hey phil when you push on the pedal to stop and the car is sitting there like at a stop light can you feel the pedal slowing going to the floor? if you can and you can't find any leaks any where i.e. where the caliper is or inside the car under the brake pedal where it goes throw the fire wall then what you have is either air in the line or more likely the master cylinder is by passing inside meaning you need a new one and you will need to bleed it extensively to get all the air out. hope this helps. Donald||||reg||6
Z||000002||stimpy||02-27-2000||11:23 PM||||I have experienced a similar prob. I don't know if it is any correlation, but after jacking the car up, I woulb notice the pedal taking a couple of pumps to get pressure. I opened the resevoir, popped out the cups on the rubber diaphragm, made sure the level was ok, put the diaphragm back in and made sure the cap was on tight. Don't know if that will wor for you, but it is nice to try the free fix before spending big bucks. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-27-2000||11:44 PM||||I'd put a different master cylinder in, rebuilt ones are not all that much.||||reg||1
Z||000004||b_b_a_d||02-27-2000||11:53 PM||||I agree with Luperman...  In the process of my wife and I rebuilding our engine, I lost plenty of brake fluid when replacing the calipers.  I still need to bleed the brakes 100%!!!  I still have air in the line with that sponge effect.  Someday soon I will find time to bleed the brakes as the car has become my wifes daily driver... that or I will have to take out a substantial life insurance policy on my wife.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>PS:  My wife is capable of engine rebuilds, so please don't think I am putting this off for her own demise.  I've been sick, but the truth of the matter is I'm really lazy and waiting for her to do it herself!  After all, She did steal my car.||||reg||11
Z||000005||Phil||02-28-2000||07:08 AM|||| The brakes have been bled several times-solid pedal just a long first stroke. That's why I was wondering if the 88 master had a larger bore or something. The prior owner did do some parts changing which got me to thinking(a dangerous thing)||||reg||1
Z||000006||lawrence||02-28-2000||08:32 AM||||If you are sure there's no air in the line, and if the brake pedal doesn't slowly move down (internal leak in m/c) then I think you have a problem with the caliper/rotor positioning.<P>There is a heck of a lot of flex in the rear axle flange that holds the rotor and wheel.  As such, the rotor tends to move in and out, especially during a turn.  This in/out motion of the rotor tends to open the space between the two brake pads.  That causes the need for increased motion to make first contact on the rotors.<P>But your Formula doesn't exhibit the symptom.  So, I would say that your other car has a loose bearing, and that's causing an extra amount of rotor movement, and therefore causing an extra amount of brake pad movement.<P>I would jack up the car, grab the wheels and see how much play you have.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||02-28-2000||08:46 AM||||Thought of the loose bearing deal earlier, I checked and all is hunky dory. It's got me stumped. I even took it to a wheel and brake shop(he owned a Fiero once) it's a bit better but still not up to snuff. The wife is giving me some static because it our sons car. Evidently my car can be a piece of rubbish but his is a different story. I guess he rates higher. Seriously though this thing is very annoying.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||02-28-2000||10:58 AM||||Phil,<P>No one has mentioned the parking brake adjustment. Maybe it's too obvious. I know on my 85 GT the brakes were just as you discribed. As it turned out, I had a bad habit of NEVER using the parking brake. As a result, the self-adjuster was never used and the rear calipers were badly out of adjustment. It required almost one full stroke of the pedal to compensate for the poor adjustment. <P>When the new pads were installed, did you manually re-adjust the rear calipers?<P>Just a thought,<BR>Roy L.||||reg||4
Z||000009||lawrence||02-28-2000||12:47 PM||||Yogi Berra said, "It's amazing what you can see by just looking".  Maybe it's time to get under the car.  Have someone press and release the brake pedal.  Then rotate the tire and see if the calipers do something funny.  If they aren't moving, then it's back to looking at the hydraulics.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000010||stsmithgt||02-28-2000||01:06 PM||||Seeing if [/b]all the calipers are working is a good idea.  I replaced mine and one of the I bought, failed.  I was frustrated because I wasn't looking for a good part to be bad when I had my brake troubles.  The piston in one of the calipers was backing off the rotor too much when the brake was released which would make me have to pump the pedal once to get it to work fine in the right position.  This sounds similar to your problem.  I hope my two cents can help. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||stsmithgt||02-28-2000||01:11 PM||||OH yeah, calipers should be replaced at least in pairs, same axle of course.||||reg||1
